---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { atlas } from '../lib/atlas.ts';
import { author } from '../lib/editorial.ts';

const a = atlas();
const me = author();
---

<Base title="About" description="Hi, I'm Mikel. I built ProductEngineer.pro to make sense of the work around writing software, and to share what I'm learning along the way.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Crumbs items={[{ label: 'About' }]} />
    <header class="page-head">
      <div class="code-big">About</div>
      <h1 class="title">A place to make sense of the work.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I love building software. Over the years, I've found myself just as interested in the questions around it:
        who needs this, what would help them, and did the thing we shipped actually work? This site is my way of
        connecting those questions and sharing what I learn.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section" id="who-writes-it">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Who writes it</h2><span class="label">Author</span></div>
      <div class="about-author">
        {me.photo && <img class="author-photo" src={me.photo} alt={me.name} width="160" height="160" />}
        <div class="prose author-intro">
          <p>
            Hi, I'm {me.name}, a full-stack engineer and tech lead from Donostia. I've been building software
            for more than fifteen years, and my own products since 2009. I still love taking an idea all the way
            to something people can use.
          </p>
          <p>
            At Majestic, I work on campaigns and platforms for global brands. On my own time, I build things like
            Linguaso, for learning languages through videos, and Etengabe, for learning Basque. Having my own
            products means I get to live with the decisions after launch, too.
          </p>
          <p>
            I made the Skills Map because I wanted to see how all these parts of the job fit together, including the ones
            I'd been doing without a name for them. It's also a reminder of how much I still want to learn.
            If it helps you find your next question, or put a name to something you already do, I'm glad you're here.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            {me.links.map((l) => <a class="btn" href={l.href} rel="me noopener">{l.label}</a>)}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>What you'll find here</h2><span class="label">Two parts</span></div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The <a href="/atlas/">Skills Map</a> is the map I wish I'd had earlier: the skills of product engineering,
            with short explanations, resources worth your time and tools to try. You can use it to get your bearings
            and find something you'd like to learn next.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <a href="/fieldwork/">In practice</a> is where I work through those skills in practice. Sometimes that
            means looking closely at someone else's product or talking to its maker. Sometimes it means changing
            one of my own, measuring what happened and writing down what I'd do differently.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Three levels</h2><span class="label">01</span></div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The Skills Map has {a.territories.length - 1} territories, plus a group of cross-cutting capabilities. Under them sit
            {a.capabilities.length} capabilities and {a.skills.length} skills. Each skill is one tile on the map and has one home;
            when it matters elsewhere, other pages link to it instead of repeating it.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is how I've organised the discipline so far. You don't need to master every tile. I use it to notice
            the areas I rarely touch and choose where I'd like to go deeper. I hope you'll find your own way through it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Codes such as <span class="mono">06.4.5</span> read territory, capability, skill. They follow the order of the
            taxonomy and are there to help you find your way. Links use permanent ids, so a renamed skill keeps its old address.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>AI and technical leverage</h2><span class="label">Across the Skills Map</span></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Relevant skills include AI workflows, limitations and verification, plus the technical knowledge that helps you implement or measure the work yourself. Practice exercises and reviewable artifacts make competence concrete.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/atlas/ai/">AI territory</a> brings together AI engineering and AI-assisted work. General verification and workflow automation remain cross-cutting. Filter the <a href="/atlas/?focus=ai">map by AI &amp; automation</a> to find them across territories. Focus filters overlap; each skill keeps one home and one progress record.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>What I choose to share</h2><span class="label">02</span></div>
      <p class="lede">I want the links here to feel like a recommendation from a colleague. These are the things I look for.</p>
      <ol class="index compact">
        {
          [
            'Written or recorded by practitioners or recognised sources, and the primary source over a summary of it.',
            'Free to read or watch. A paid book or course appears only when it is the standard reference, and is marked as paid.',
            'Still accurate, in English, and dated.',
            'No link is written from memory. Each one is opened and checked before it is saved, and a link checker re-runs every week.',
            'Tools: widely used ones, with an open-source or free option where one exists. No affiliate links.',
            'Two good links beat a full quota, so some topics have fewer.',
          ].map((t, i) => (
            <li>
              <div class="row" style="grid-template-columns:40px minmax(0,1fr)">
                <span class="code">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <span style="font-size:16px">{t}</span>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="fieldwork">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>How I write for In practice</h2><span class="label">03</span></div>
      <p class="lede">I want these pieces to be useful to someone facing a similar decision. That means showing how I got there, including what didn't work.</p>
      <ol class="index compact">
        {
          [
            'Every entry says how I looked: version, platform, dates, time spent and what I left out.',
            'What I saw stays apart from what I guess about other users.',
            'Each kind of entry follows the same sections, so you can compare one with another.',
            'Experiments record the hypothesis, the metric and the guardrails before the result.',
            'Failed and inconclusive experiments are published with the same detail as the ones that worked.',
            'Interviewees read their interview before it goes out. No sponsored reviews and no affiliate links.',
            'Every entry names the Skills Map skills it used, so the map shows where the evidence is and where it is not.',
          ].map((t, i) => (
            <li>
              <div class="row" style="grid-template-columns:40px minmax(0,1fr)">
                <span class="code">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <span style="font-size:16px">{t}</span>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="privacy">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Your progress and privacy</h2><span class="label">04</span></div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            You can mark each skill as learning, comfortable or not interested. Without an account that status stays in your browser and nothing
            is sent to a server. Export a file from <a href="/me/#data">your account page</a> to keep a copy.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you sign in with GitHub or Google, your statuses are saved to an account so they follow you to any device. The
            account holds your provider id, your name, your avatar, the email address shared by your provider and your statuses. You can
            delete the account, and everything in it, from the <a href="/me/">account page</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            AI agents can read the Skills Map through an <a href="/me/#agents">MCP server</a>. An agent can read your statuses only after
            you approve it, and it can never change them.
          </p>
          <p>Page analytics, when enabled, are cookieless and collect no personal data. Videos load from YouTube only when you press play.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            A link can light any set of skills and join them with a route, for example
            <a href="/atlas/?lit=user-interviews,hypotheses,sample-size,funnel-analysis">user interviews to funnels</a>. It shows
            which parts of the discipline a piece of work exercised. Every <a href="/fieldwork/">In practice</a>
            entry does this for the skills it used.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
